# Papers read but not yet recorded, and why. Kept so that "not extracted" is a
# decision on the record rather than an oversight.
#
2103.13106v2  PbWO4, 4 crystals 25x25x10 mm3, in the LCE-correcting subset.
              Text gives yields only as "few hundred photons/MeV"; per-crystal
              values are in a table that needs re-reading. A placeholder was
              briefly entered and removed: no guessed number belongs in the
              records file. Notable regardless -- only the COUPLING FACE is
              stated to be polished, the other five faces are not described, and
              the crystals are attached to the photocathode without the coupling
              medium being named.
1806.02461v2  SoLid detector optimisation. EXCLUDED at full text: the
              scintillator is PVT plastic, which the protocol excludes. The
              title/abstract screen did not catch it.
1411.6246v1   CsI cryogenic study. EXCLUDED at full text: quotes other people's
              absolute yields (60,000 and 100,000 ph/MeV) but measures only
              relative temperature dependence.
0910.4414v1   CaWO4 non-proportionality. EXCLUDED: relative light yield in keVee
              at millikelvin temperatures, no absolute photon yield.
0805.2446v1   PANDA PWO thesis. EXCLUDED: the 10 and 20 phe/MeV figures are
              quoted as "approximate properties" of the supplied crystals, not
              measured in the work.
1203.1172v2   NaI and NaI(Tl) at low temperature. EXCLUDED: light yield reported
              only relative to its own room-temperature value.
2605.28414v1  GYGAG / Gd2-xYxSi2O7 ceramics for neutrinoless double beta decay.
              NOT recorded: the ~57,000 photons/MeV figure appears as motivation
              quoted from the literature for GPS, and I could not establish from
              the sections read that the ceramic samples of this work were
              measured to it. Attributing it would be a guess. Worth returning
              to -- the paper is otherwise informative, and notes that the Teflon
              tape was REMOVED from the top surface for the alpha measurements,
              so one crystal was measured in two reflector configurations within
              a single paper.
1509.07822v2  Cs2NaRBr3I3 elpasolites. NOT recorded yet: the light yield was
              measured by coupling a sample to a Hamamatsu R6321-100 PMT and
              recording pulse-height spectra, but the sections read do not say
              what it was normalised against. Needs the reference standard
              before a row can be honest about reference_material.
2408.11070v1  Sc/Y silicate, intrinsic RL 12,000 ph/MeV. NOT recorded yet: same
              reason -- the normalisation is not established from the sections
              read. The paper is separately interesting because its subject is
              that the yield DEPENDS ON EXCITATION DENSITY (a 20x beam flux
              change shifts it), which is another axis on which a single
              "light yield" is not a single number.
1612.06071v2  CsI(Na) at low temperature. EXCLUDED: absolute yields quoted are
              other people's (under 5,000 ph/MeV at RT, 100,000 at 77 K).
2410.09371v1  Thin GAGG:Ce plates. NOT recorded yet, but note for the paper: it
              states LuAG:Ce measured "within the 1 microsecond time window is
              as low as 12,500 ph/MeV" while the integrated radioluminescence is
              notably higher, and that Dorenbos' theoretical ceiling is 60,000.
              Integration window is therefore a further axis on which one
              material has several published "light yields".
2110.01992v2  Ca(Br,I)2 pulse-shape discrimination study. EXCLUDED as a
              measurement: the light outputs it states (107,000 ph/MeV for CaI2,
              51,000 for CaBrI, 79,000 for CaBrI:Eu) are carried over from the
              group's earlier growth paper, not measured here. Worth noting for
              the survey anyway: the crystals were "connected directly via
              optical grease (6262A) WITHOUT REFLECTORS" -- an explicit statement
              of no wrapping, which is rarer than it should be, and which makes
              those yields not comparable with the wrapped measurements that
              dominate the literature.
2110.06618v1  GAGG(Ce) response to charged particles. NOT recorded: the 40,000
              ph/MeV in the text is the value the paper quotes for NaI(Tl) as a
              comparison, not the GAGG(Ce) yield this work measures. Needs
              re-reading. Worth returning to: the crystal surface is FROSTED and
              wrapped in 65 micron ESR film, and the shaping time is scanned
              over 0.5, 1.0 and 2.0 microseconds rather than fixed -- both axes
              this survey cares about, both explicitly reported.
1904.03640v2  Ce:La-GPS pulse-shape discrimination. EXCLUDED as a measurement:
              Table 1 is headed "Basic properties of La-GPS:Ce [5]" and is
              cited, not measured here. Kept in the notes because of HOW it is
              cited: the light output of the material is given as a RANGE,
              "38,000 - 48,000 photons/MeV", a 26 percent band presented as a
              basic property of the material. The setup is otherwise well
              described (Hamamatsu R9869 PMT, Shin-Etsu KF-96H-100 optical
              grease).
2507.09765v2  CRYSP total-body PET with cryogenic CsI. NOT recorded: a design
              and simulation paper rather than a yield measurement. Useful for
              its explicitness about geometry: monolithic crystals 48x48x37 mm3,
              ALL surfaces polished, FIVE OF SIX faces wrapped in PTFE for
              reflectivity above 95 percent, and the wrapping itself adds about
              2 mm to the transverse dimensions. The authors state plainly that
              "full wrapping with a good reflector is a must for energy
              resolution".
2001.06672v1  Ce and Pr doped lutetium silicates, trapping study. NOT recorded:
              the 26,000-30,000 ph/MeV quoted is background from the literature,
              and the work measures trapping rather than absolute yield.
2203.13322v1  Blue-emitting scintillating fibres, timing study. EXCLUDED: no
              absolute yield; fibres are diamond polished and read by SiPMs.
1710.11233v3  Light guides and wavelength-shifting plates for liquid argon.
              EXCLUDED: the 40,000 photons/MeV figure is an ASSUMED input to
              simulations, cited to four references, not a measurement.
2609.09640v1  Simulated gamma spectroscopy with CeBr3. EXCLUDED as a
              measurement, but a clean illustration of the circularity this
              survey is about: it SETS the intrinsic yield to 6.0e4 photons/MeV
              because that is "reported for bare crystals", then models the
              crystal with a PTFE-like diffuse wrapping of about 94 percent
              reflectivity. A simulation takes a published yield as input; the
              published yield depended on somebody's wrapping.
1008.2175v1   Structure and optical properties of halide scintillators.
              EXCLUDED: an electronic-structure paper; the ~100,000 ph/MeV and
              55,000 ph/MeV figures are quoted from others.
